------------AI Response-------------
The Builder Pattern is a creational design pattern that allows you to construct complex objects step-by-step. It separates the construction of an object from its representation, allowing the same construction process to create different representations.
An easy way to understand this is ordering a custom pizza at a restaurant:
⚬	Instead of having fixed pizzas on the menu, you start with a basic crust.
⚬	You tell the chef step-by-step: add tomato sauce, add mozzarella, add pepperoni, but no mushrooms.
⚬	Finally, the chef "builds" and delivers your specific pizza. You don't pay for or handle ingredients you didn't ask for.
Why Do We Use It?
⚬	Eliminates "Constructor Hell" (Telescoping Constructors): Without Builder, a class with many optional fields requires dozens of overloaded constructors (e.g., Pizza(size), Pizza(size, cheese), Pizza(size, cheese, pepperoni)). This quickly becomes unmaintainable.
⚬	Improves Code Readability: Constructor calls with multiple parameters of the same type—like new House(4, 2, true, false, true)—are error-prone and hard to read. The Builder pattern uses readable method chaining like .setDoors(4).setHasGarage(true).
⚬	Supports Object Immutability: You can make the target object fully immutable (all fields final with no setter methods) because all values are passed to the builder before the actual object is instantiated.
⚬	Encapsulates Construction Logic: Validation or complex assembly logic is kept inside the builder, keeping your domain models clean and focused.
When Should We Use It?
⚬	Many Optional Parameters: When an object has a few required parameters but a large number of optional ones.
⚬	Immutable Objects: When you need to create immutable objects whose state shouldn't change after creation.
⚬	Complex Object Assembly: When creating an object involves multiple steps, validations, or precise sequencing.
⚬	Alternative to Large Constructors: When a constructor's parameter list grows past 4–5 arguments, making parameter order easy to confuse.
Code Example (Java)
// Target Class: Immutable Pizza object
public class Pizza {
    // Required parameters
    private final String size;
    private final String crust;

    // Optional parameters
    private final boolean cheese;
    private final boolean pepperoni;
    private final boolean bacon;
    private final boolean mushrooms;

    // Private constructor: Only the Builder can create instances of Pizza
    private Pizza(Builder builder) {
        this.size = builder.size;
        this.crust = builder.crust;
        this.cheese = builder.cheese;
        this.pepperoni = builder.pepperoni;
        this.bacon = builder.bacon;
        this.mushrooms = builder.mushrooms;
    }

    // Getters only (No setters -> Immutability)
    public String getSize() { return size; }
    public String getCrust() { return crust; }
    public boolean hasCheese() { return cheese; }
    public boolean hasPepperoni() { return pepperoni; }
    public boolean hasBacon() { return bacon; }
    public boolean hasMushrooms() { return mushrooms; }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "Pizza [" + size + " " + crust + " crust, Cheese=" + cheese +
               ", Pepperoni=" + pepperoni + ", Bacon=" + bacon + ", Mushrooms=" + mushrooms + "]";
    }

    // Static Inner Builder Class
    public static class Builder {
        // Required parameters
        private final String size;
        private final String crust;

        // Optional parameters - initialized to default values
        private boolean cheese = false;
        private boolean pepperoni = false;
        private boolean bacon = false;
        private boolean mushrooms = false;

        // Builder Constructor for REQUIRED fields
        public Builder(String size, String crust) {
            this.size = size;
            this.crust = crust;
        }

        // Methods for OPTIONAL fields (Return 'this' to allow method chaining)
        public Builder addCheese() {
            this.cheese = true;
            return this;
        }

        public Builder addPepperoni() {
            this.pepperoni = true;
            return this;
        }

        public Builder addBacon() {
            this.bacon = true;
            return this;
        }

        public Builder addMushrooms() {
            this.mushrooms = true;
            return this;
        }

        // Final step: Construct and return the actual Pizza object
        public Pizza build() {
            return new Pizza(this);
        }
    }
}

// Client Code
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Building a Meat Lover's Pizza
        Pizza meatLovers = new Pizza.Builder("Large", "Thick")
                .addCheese()
                .addPepperoni()
                .addBacon()
                .build();

        // Building a Veggie/Simple Pizza
        Pizza simpleVeggie = new Pizza.Builder("Medium", "Thin")
                .addCheese()
                .addMushrooms()
                .build();

        System.out.println(meatLovers);
        System.out.println(simpleVeggie);
    }
}
